The Bluetooth half of the UWE5622 never reaches BlueZ on a 6.18 kernel, and the
only trace it leaves is one line:

	Bluetooth: hci0: Opcode 0x080f failed: -22

0x080f is WRITE_DEF_LINK_POLICY, sent once by init stage 3. -22 is -EINVAL,
which is what bt_to_errno() makes of HCI error 0x12: the controller answers
"Invalid HCI Command Parameters", whatever the policy derived from its own LMP
features is. The vendor's 4.9 tree never cared -- a request built with
hci_req_add() carried on after a command it did not like -- but hci_sync does:

	err = stage[i].func(hdev);
	if (err)
		return err;          /* hci_init_stage_sync() */

so the sequence stops there, hci_init() fails, hci_dev_open_sync() fails, and
hci_power_on_sync() returns before the two things it does at the end:

	if (hci_dev_test_and_clear_flag(hdev, HCI_SETUP)) {
		...
		mgmt_index_added(hdev);
	}

HCI_SETUP stays set, and read_index_list() skips such a device, so the
management interface reports no controllers at all. bluetoothd is then left with
an empty list, registers nothing under /org/bluez/hci0, and has nothing to
complain about: `btmgmt info` says "Index list with 0 items" while
/sys/class/bluetooth/hci0 is right there, and every D-Bus lookup of the adapter
path answers UnknownObject.

A default link policy is a BR/EDR convention about role switch and sniff. It is
not worth a controller that is invisible to every program on the board, so a
rejected one is now noted and stepped over, as the older kernels did.

--- a/net/bluetooth/hci_sync.c
+++ b/net/bluetooth/hci_sync.c
@@ -4335,6 +4335,7 @@
 {
 	struct hci_cp_write_def_link_policy cp;
 	u16 link_policy = 0;
+	int err;
 
 	if (!(hdev->commands[5] & 0x10))
 		return 0;
@@ -4352,8 +4353,23 @@
 
 	cp.policy = cpu_to_le16(link_policy);
 
-	return __hci_cmd_sync_status(hdev, HCI_OP_WRITE_DEF_LINK_POLICY,
-				     sizeof(cp), &cp, HCI_CMD_TIMEOUT);
+	err = __hci_cmd_sync_status(hdev, HCI_OP_WRITE_DEF_LINK_POLICY,
+				    sizeof(cp), &cp, HCI_CMD_TIMEOUT);
+
+	/*
+	 * The UWE5622 answers this with "Invalid HCI Command Parameters"
+	 * whatever policy is asked for. That used to cost nothing: a request
+	 * built with hci_req_add() went on to its next command after a failed
+	 * one. It now ends the init sequence, and with it the open, so the
+	 * controller never leaves HCI_SETUP -- a flag the management layer
+	 * hides, which is why no controller is ever reported to BlueZ.
+	 */
+	if (err == -EINVAL) {
+		bt_dev_warn(hdev, "default link policy rejected, continuing");
+		return 0;
+	}
+
+	return err;
 }
 
 static int hci_read_page_scan_activity_sync(struct hci_dev *hdev)
